{"title":"1986 USAMO Problems/Problem 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1986_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/f/b9f142ba01875edaac4128763a9e91f0642fc8c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\text{a})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"18\" /> Do there exist 14 consecutive positive integers each of which is divisible by one or more primes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> from the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/7/167e92076fedc7b470983d6607fa9249d372b510.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\le p \\le 11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"83\" height=\"15\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/6/666259a59c9810eadd068d218ed8b663b5387e43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\text{b})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"18\" /> Do there exist 21 consecutive positive integers each of which is divisible by one or more primes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> from the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/d/41df8f820b98008f277d2b88be504ba89b94c83e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\le p \\le 13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"84\" height=\"16\" />?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1986_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(a)     To solve part (a), we first note that for any 14 consecutive positive integers, exactly 7 are even (divisible by 2) and therefore satisfy the criteria. We can remove these from the problem, and simplify it to the following question, which is equivalent to part (a):\n\"Do there exist 7 consecutive positive odd integers each of which is divisible by one or more primes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> from the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/8/b18aee1fadbbfe705fdbc3c7ce5c1f0d4ffd54cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\le p \\le 11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"83\" height=\"16\" />?\"\nAmong any 7 consecutive positive odd integers, the following holds:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/7/e97ecf844d1c1c61a269b7f30400a02048b078b7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Either 2 or 3 are divisible by 3}\\]\" width=\"222\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/d/84da2a95fc2815225b25e101bc115641fffc7c55.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Either 1 or 2 are divisible by 5}\\]\" width=\"222\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/5/ed55b7c956501dab07d9803ffba8aca529b70f39.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Exactly 1 is divisible by 7}\\]\" width=\"187\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/e/bfe5b047480fc9b5705de80106b8df954070bba0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Either 0 or 1 are divisible by 11}\\]\" width=\"230\" height=\"15\" />\nFor every one of these seven integers to be divisible by one of 3, 5, 7, or 11, there must be 3 multiples of 3, 2 multiples of 5, 1 multiple of 7, and 1 multiple of 11. Additionally, none of these integers may be a multiple of any two of the four aforementioned primes. Otherwise, the Pigeonhole Principle dictates that at least one integer is not divisible by any of the four, thus failing to meet the criteria. But this cannot be. Calling the consecutive odd integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/0/310f72e4a725e4590dcf2dca67597ee57b285288.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"98\" height=\"11\" />, we note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/b/1ab39d761413804680d26d972381f028001562f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/2/5e28850813a5321d2a8ae3c0510457a7337975ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/2/012aa2573a77f1bfe01a31fdebe941ee94b10808.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> must be multiples of 3, and therefore cannot be multiples of 5, 7, or 11. But for there to be two multiples of 5, they must be one of the two pairs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/e/6fed9dddfa148f740d71184b83b5fbcc9184049f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_1,a_6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/c/20c35017bc9191d96afcac2026d02f2edcd9b15a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_2,a_7)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" />. But each of these pairs contains a multiple of 3, and so at least one of the 7 odd integers is divisible by none of the primes 3, 5, 7, or 11. Therefore the answer to part (a) is no.\n</p><p>(b)    To solve part (b), we use a strategy similar to the one used in part (a), reducing part (b) to this equivalent question:\n\"Do there exist 10 consecutive positive odd integers each of which is divisible by one or more primes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> from the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/b/3fbaba4ade9f39f4fedc2dd247cfff5dd7be2f02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\le p \\le 13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"84\" height=\"16\" />?\"\n(We will ignore the case where the first of the 21 integers is odd, resulting in 11 odd integers instead of 10, as it is only true if the weaker, 10-integer argument is also true.)\nAmong any 10 consecutive positive odd integers, the following holds:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/2/822d2b69ed3404a252a3e98703c0bb5a42f55037.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Either 3 or 4 are divisible by 3}\\]\" width=\"222\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/6/e769927d5200ba316bba935703b03a64e8586275.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Exactly 2 are divisible by 5}\\]\" width=\"197\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/f/78f9b5877da720d88cef3c88859b6dc49040f9cb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Either 1 or 2 are divisible by 7}\\]\" width=\"223\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/e/bfe5b047480fc9b5705de80106b8df954070bba0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Either 0 or 1 are divisible by 11}\\]\" width=\"230\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/f/e4f4e23f0c578b9695899118d04fc34225994edd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Either 0 or 1 are divisible by 13}\\]\" width=\"231\" height=\"15\" />\nFor every one of these ten integers to be divisible by one of 3, 5, 7, 11, or 13, there must be 4 multiples of 3, 2 multiples of 5, 2 multiples of 7, 1 multiple of 11, and 1 multiple of 13. As before, none of these integers may be a multiple of any two of the five primes. Calling the consecutive odd integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/f/a7f7fd88f01f57c51ccece47f402d57c4d8b6647.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"13\" />, we note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/b/1ab39d761413804680d26d972381f028001562f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/2/5e28850813a5321d2a8ae3c0510457a7337975ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/2/012aa2573a77f1bfe01a31fdebe941ee94b10808.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/5/f659be762622558799a9119468b5008cb1b27f78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"22\" height=\"10\" /> must be multiples of 3, and therefore cannot be multiples of 5, 7, 11, or 13. Unlike part (a), however, this stipulation is not a dead end. Let the multiples of 5 be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/2/572b65ebc3f438b176b4ca4a890799f2f72564af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/a/7ead075983f8dbfa721f00c28be967778b4a6677.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> and let the multiples of 7 be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/9/5e97a8af68fbc8e357d3ee0eba452022b06c1875.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/7/fb7904c50fdc103fee44ab630f82383c0b78febc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" />. The multiples of 11 and 13 are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/5/0d5d34d49a7eb6afd4874d7bdaf23946dbf63cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/a/34ace040475607683853fcfd4960a8f7fa6678c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" />, in some order (it doesn't really matter which). An example of a sequence of 21 consecutive positive integers satisfying part (b) is the integers from 9440 to 9460 (inclusive), which can be obtained by solving modular equations that result from these statements. So the answer to part (b) is yes.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1986_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1986_USAMO\" title=\"1986 USAMO\">1986 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1986_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"1986 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=1986\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b>First<br />Problem</b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1986_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1986 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1986_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1986 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1986_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1986 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1986_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1986 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1986_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1986 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017022650\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.030 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.054 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 219/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1240/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 54/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 6474/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   18.140     